<span style="color:blue">**Claim 1.**</span>  $\measuredangle{PCY}=\measuredangle{PX'Y}+\measuredangle{CX'P}-90$ *Proof.* We do some angle chasing.
\begin{align*}
 \measuredangle{PCY}&=\measuredangle{PCA}=\measuredangle{PBA}=90-\measuredangle{ZPB}=90-(\measuredangle{ZPA}+\measuredangle{APB})  &=90-(\measuredangle{ZPA}+\measuredangle{ACB})=90-(180-\measuredangle{CX'Y})=\measuredangle{CX'Y}-90
\end{align*}


<span style="color:blue">**Claim 2.**</span>  $YX'CP$  is cyclic.
*Proof.* Since  $\measuredangle{PCY}=90-\measuredangle{YPC}$  we have
\begin{align*}
 90-\measuredangle{YPC}&=\measuredangle{CX'Y}-90 
 &\implies \measuredangle{CX'Y}+\measuredangle{YPC}=180 
 &\implies YX'CP \text{ is cyclic}
\end{align*}

Now since  $YX'CP$  is cyclic, we know that  $\measuredangle{CX'P}=\measuredangle{CYP}=90^\circ \implies X'=X$  and we are done.

We accordingly compute:
\begin{align*}
&\measuredangle PXY\stackrel{(PCXY)}{=}\measuredangle PCY\stackrel{A,C,Y}{=}\measuredangle PCA
&\measuredangle PXZ\stackrel{(PBXZ)}{=}\measuredangle PBZ\stackrel{A,B,Z}{=}\measuredangle PBA,
\end{align*}
of which the latter two are equal because  $PABC$  is cyclic. So by transitivity  $\measuredangle PXY=\measuredangle PXZ$  or  $X$ ,  $Y$ , and  $Z$  are collinear, and we may conclude.  $\blacksquare$ [https://www.geogebra.org/calculator/ekgya62k](https://www.geogebra.org/calculator/ekgya62k)

