Let  $D$  be the second intersection of the line  $\overleftrightarrow{SQ}$  and  $(ABC)$ .
Note that  $PQMF$  cyclic if and only if  $\measuredangle{AFD}=\measuredangle{CDF}$ . It is equivalent of showing  $\overline{AB}\parallel\overline{CD}$ .
Let  $(ABC)$  be a unit circle, and  $a,b,c,f,s,d$  be corresponding complex numbers of the points  $A,B,C,F,S$ , and  $D$ , respectively. Without loss of generality, let  $c=1$ .
We may show that if  $D$  is an intersection of the parallel line of  $\overleftrightarrow{AB}$  through  $C$  with  $(ABC)$ , then  $F,S,D$  are collinear. First, notice that  $$ s=\frac{a+b+c}{3}=\frac{a+b+1}{3} $$  Since  $\overline{CF}\perp\overline{AB}$  and  $F\in \overline{AB}$ ,  $$ \frac{f-a}{a-b}-\frac{\overline{f}-\frac{1}{a}}{\frac{1}{a}-\frac{1}{b}}=0\implies f-a+ab\overline{f}-b=0 $$    $$ \frac{f-1}{a-b}+\frac{\overline{f}-1}{\frac{1}{a}-\frac{1}{b}}=0\implies f-1-ab\overline{f}+ab=0 $$  Therefore,  $f=\dfrac{a+b+1-ab}{2}$ . The point  $D$  is defined by  $|d|=1$ ,  $d\neq 1$  and  $\overline{DC}\perp \overline{FC}$ . So,  $$ \frac{d-1}{f-1}+\frac{\frac{1}{d}-1}{\overline{f}-1}=0\implies d=\frac{f-1}{\overline{f}-1}=\frac{a+b-1-ab}{\frac{1}{a}+\frac{1}{b}-1-\frac{1}{ab}}=ab. $$  Consider  $$ \frac{d-s}{d-f}=\frac{ab-\frac{a+b+1}{3}}{ab-\frac{a+b+1-ab}{2}}=\frac{2}{3}\left(\frac{3ab-a-b-1}{3ab-a-b-1}\right)=\frac{2}{3}\in\mathbb{R}. $$  Which implies  $D,S$  and  $F$  are collinear. So, the proof is complete.

