<details><summary>Click to expand</summary>The answer is   $$ d[G,A]>d[A,Q]>d[H,G], $$   where  $d[x,y]$  denotes the length of the intervall  $[x,y]$ .
We will get this by proving the inequalities 
\begin{align*}
&\text{(1)}  \; d[G,A] > d[H,G], 
&\text{(2)}  \; d[G,A] > d[A,Q], \;\;\;
&\text{(3)}  \; d[A,Q] > d[H,G]. 
\end{align*}
We will use the following two "theorems" about the numbers  $H$ ,  $G$ ,  $A$  and  $Q$ :
\begin{align*}
&\text{(I)} \; AH=G^2, 
&\text{(II)} \; G^2+Q^2=2A^2,
\end{align*} or in other words,**(I)**  $G$  is the geometric mean of  $A$  and  $H$ , and **(II)**  $A$  is the quadratic mean of  $G$  and  $Q$ .

The proofs of these "theorems" are trivial.**(1)** Using this, we get   $$ G= GM[H,A] < AM[H,A], $$   because  $H<A$ , and we see that  $AM[H,A]$  lies exactly between the numbers  $H$  and  $A$ , hence   $$ d[G,A] > d[H,G]. $$  **(2)** Furthermore, we get   $$ A= QM[G,Q] > AM[G,Q], $$   because  $G<Q$ , and we see that  $AM[G,Q]$  lies exactly between the numbers  $G$  and  $Q$ , hence   $$ d[G,A] > d[A,Q]. $$  **(3)** Now we have to prove that  $d[A,Q] > d[H,G]$ .

To do so, we would need to proof that  $d[G,Q] > d[H,A]$ . This follows from
\begin{align*}
2A(Q-G) &= 2QM[Q,G](Q-G)  &> 2AM[Q,G](Q-G)  &= (Q+G)(Q-G)=Q^2-G^2 
&=(2A^2-G^2)-G^2=2A^2-2G^2  &=2A^2-2AH=2A(A-H).
\end{align*}
Hence, the proof is done.</details>

