Proof: Let the incircle be denoted by  $\Gamma$  and circumcircle be denoted as  $\omega$ . For any point  $P$  in the plane, let  $f(P) = \mathrm{pow}_{\Gamma}(P) - \mathrm{pow}_{\omega}(P)$ . So, we know that  $f$  is a linear function on any line.

Then, it is enough to show that  $f(X) = f(Y)$ , since in that case  $XY$  will be parallel to the radical axis of  $\omega$  and  $\Gamma$ , and hence perpendicular to  $OI$ .
Let  $x = s-a, \ y = s-b, \ z = s-c$ . We have   $$ f(A) = (s-a)^2 = x^2, \ f(B) = (s-b)^2 = y^2, \ \implies f(X) = (s-b)^2\cdot \frac{(c-a)}{c} + (s-a)^2 \cdot \frac{a}{c} = \frac{y^2(x-z) + x^2(y+z)}{x+y} = xy + xz - yz. $$   Interchanging  $y,z$  will give us the expression for  $f(Y)$  by symmetry, therefore   $$ f(Y) = xy + xz - yz = f(X), $$   and we are done.  $\square$

