Here's a solution with Barycentric Coordinates:**<span style="color:#741B47">Rephrased Problem:</span>** Let  $\displaystyle \triangle ABC$  be a triangle with circumcentre  $O$  and Incentre  $I$ , and suppose  $X,Y$  are points on rays  $\overrightarrow{BA}, \overrightarrow{CA}$  respectively, such that  $BX = CY = AB$ . Prove that  $OI \perp XY$ .

Proof: We will use homogeneous barycentric coordinates. We have  $A = (1:0:0), \ B = (0:1:0), \ C = (0:0:1)$ . Now,  $X = (a:c-a:0), \ Y = (a:0:b-a)$ . Thus,   $$ \overleftrightarrow{XY}: (c-a)(b-a)x - a(b-a)y - a(c-a)z = 0. $$   For any line  $ux + vy + wz = 0$ , we have the infinite point as  $(v-w: w-u: u-v)$ . Thus, we have   $$ \infty_{XY} = (a(b-c): b(c-a): c(a-b)). $$  Now it is well known that  $O = (a^2S_A:b^2S_B:c^2S_C), \ I = (a:b:c)$ . Thus,   $$ \overleftrightarrow{OI}: bc(cS_C - bS_B) x + ca(aS_A - cS_C) y + ab(bS_B - aS_A)z = 0 $$  Note that  $cS_C - bS_B = 2s(s-a)(b-c)$ , therefore, we have   $$ \overleftrightarrow{OI}: sbc(s-a)(b-c) x + sca(s-b)(c-a) y + sab(s-c)(a-b) z = 0 $$  Also,  $sca(s-b)(c-a) - sab(s-c)(a-b) = a(asS_A - S^2)$ , thus   $$ \infty_{OI} = (a(asS_A - S^2): b(bsS_B - S^2): c(csS_C - S^2)). $$  Finally, for two infinite points, the directions corresponding to them are perpendicular if and only if their isogonal conjugates are antipodes on the circumcircle.
Therefore it suffices to prove that   $$  \begin{vmatrix}
a^2S_A & b^2S_B & c^2S_C
\frac{a}{b-c} & \frac{b}{c-a} & \frac{c}{a-b}
\frac{a}{asS_A - S^2} & \frac{b}{bsS_B - S^2} & \frac{c}{csS_C - S^2}
\end{vmatrix} = 0 \iff \begin{vmatrix}
(b-c)aS_A & (c-a)bS_B & (a-b)cS_C
1 & 1 & 1
\frac{b-c}{asS_A - S^2} & \frac{c-a}{bsS_B - S^2} & \frac{a-b}{csS_C - S^2}
\end{vmatrix} = 0 $$  So we need to show that   $$ \sum_{cyc} (b-c)(c-a)\left(\frac{aS_A}{bsS_B- S^2} - \frac{bS_B}{asS_A - S^2} \right) = 0 $$  We have  $aS_A(asS_A - S^2) - bS_B(bsS_B - S^2) = (aS_A - bS_B)(asS_A + bsS_B - S^2) = 2s(s-c)(b-a)(asS_A + bsS_B - S^2)$ , thus we are reduced to showing that   $$ \sum_{cyc} (s-c)(S^2 - s(aS_A+ bS_B))(S^2 - csS_C) = 0. $$  Now, \begin{align*}
\sum_{cyc} (s-c)(S^2 - s(aS_A+ bS_B))(S^2 - csS_C) &= \sum_{cyc} (s-c)( S^4 - S^2s(aS_A + bS_B + cS_C) + s^2cS_C(aS_A + bS_B)) 
&= S^4s - S^2s^2(aS_A + bS_B + cS_C) + s^2 (\sum_{cyc} ac(s-c)S_{AC} + \sum_{cyc} bc(s-c)S_{BC}) 
&= S^4s - S^2s^2(aS_A + bS_B + cS_C) + s^2 (\sum_{cyc} ac(s-c + s-a)S_{AC}) 
&= S^4s - S^2s^2(aS_A + bS_B + cS_C) + S^2s^2abc.
\end{align*}
Thus, it reduces to proving that  $s(aS_A+bS_B + cS_C) = S^2 + abcs$  which is readily verified to be true.
